{"type":"assistant","uuid":"36e8d550-8182-445f-b3e4-073c63b3ff4e","session_id":"d17bf562-7d49-4a82-98df-86bfce13ff17","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"36e8d550-8182-445f-b3e4-073c63b3ff4e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the issue clearly. Looking at the `showStockDialog` method:\n\n```java\nvoid showStockDialog(int ref) {\n    NumberPickerBuilder npb =\n            new NumberPickerBuilder()\n                    .setMinNumber(BigDecimal.ONE)\n                    .setDecimalVisibility(NumberPicker.VISIBLE)\n                    .setPlusMinusVisibility(NumberPicker.INVISIBLE)\n                    .setFragmentManager(getChildFragmentManager())\n                    .setTargetFragment(this).setReference(ref)\n                    .setStyleResId(R.style.BetterPickersDialogFragment_Calendula);\n    npb.show();\n}\n```\n\nThe code is using `getChildFragmentManager()` along with `setTargetFragment(this)`. The error occurs because the `NumberPickerDialogFragment` is declaring a target fragment that doesn't belong to the same FragmentManager hierarchy. This happens when the target fragment is managed by a different FragmentManager than the one being used to show the dialog. The fragment is being hosted in a ViewPager within the MedicinesActivity, which means it's likely a nested fragment managed by the activity's FragmentManager rather than the child fragment manager. Let me trace through the stack trace to understand the error flow—the crash occurs when the fragment manager validates that the target fragment belongs to its hierarchy, which fails because the dialog fragment was shown with `getChildFragmentManager()` but references a parent fragment manager.\n\nThe solution is to use `getParentFragment().getChildFragmentManager()` instead, which ensures the dialog fragment belongs to the same manager hierarchy as the target fragment. Or I could use the activity's fragment manager directly if the dialog shouldn't be nested at all. Let me check which approach fits the fragment structure better. `MedicineCreateOrEditFragment` is a nested fragment inside a ViewPager, so using `getParentFragment().getChildFragmentManager()` should give us the correct FragmentManager that owns both the dialog and the target fragment, resolving the mismatch. The `getParentFragment()` call is safe here because this fragment is always added as a child through `MedicinesActivity`, so it won't be null. Using the parent's child fragment manager ensures the dialog is managed by the same manager that owns the `MedicineCreateOrEditFragment`, resolving the fragmentation issue.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
